WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.530
Sony's Spider-verse is growing fast, and part
of that expanding family is a little movie

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:08.710
about a highly impressionable pile of alien
goo who finds a humanoid buddy and changes

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:09.200
his life.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.720
Venom was pulled straight from the pages of
Marvel's most 1990s comic books, and true

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:16.789
comic fans were scouring every frame for Easter
eggs.

00:00:16.789 --> 00:00:17.720
So, what did we find?

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:19.560
John Jameson, astronaut?

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:24.019
Within the first few moments of Venom, we
see a spaceship piloted by some ill-fated

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.240
members of the Life Foundation crash to Earth.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.919
As local rescue workers dig through the wreckage,
they find an astronaut they call "Jameson."

00:00:30.919 --> 00:00:35.080
While there's no more context given about
who this guy might be, there's only one astronaut

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:40.010
named Jameson in Marvel Comics : John Jameson,
the son of Daily Bugle publisher, and occasional

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:42.160
New York City mayor, J. Jonah Jameson.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.060
John spends some time in the comics as the
mighty Man-Wolf, and later, Stargod.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.300
Seeing Man-Wolf appear in Sony's rumored Morbius
movie would be a Halloween treat better than

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:54.580
a bowl of unguarded king-sized Twix, so let's
hope that this was just some lesser astronaut

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:55.840
also named Jameson.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:57.240
The Daily Globe

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:02.160
Eddie Brock's old employer, the Daily Globe,
isn't just some off-brand newspaper name tossed

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:03.460
into Sony's Spider-verse.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:07.680
Within comic book continuity, the Daily Globe
is the very place the Eddie Brock is fired

00:01:07.689 --> 00:01:10.140
from after he messes up a story on the Sin-Eater.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:14.300
While the Globe is only mentioned by name
once over dinner, it does come up again later

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:15.480
as Eddie searches for work.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.820
One of the people he texts is Barney Bushkin,
the publisher of the Globe, who probably fired

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.539
Brock in the first place for his crimes against
journalism.

00:01:22.539 --> 00:01:26.950
Bushkin carries a long-running grudge against
rival publisher J. Jonah Jameson, once even

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:31.259
abducting him with a robot and threatening
him with a fake gun in Peter Parker: The Spectacular

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.900
Spider-Man Annual #1.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:34.960
Bushkin is clearly not a guy to mess with.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:36.160
She's a lady

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:40.920
Once the name Anne Weying popped onto Venom's
cast of characters, symbiote enthusiasts the

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:45.280
world over wondered if she'd follow comic
book continuity and slip into something slithery.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:49.799
She-Venom fans rejoice, because Anne Weying
does indeed briefly appear as a female version

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:50.300
of Venom.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:51.460
That's not a good thing, though.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.040
In the Venom : Sinner Takes All miniseries,
the symbiote bonds with her to save her life…

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:59.140
but also takes her on an incredibly violent
killing spree.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:03.660
Eddie, ever supportive, assures her that she
was probably more responsible than Venom for

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:04.160
the mess.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:08.840
So, by the time Amazing Spider-Man #2 comes
around, the memory of the experience is so

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.940
traumatizing that she climbs a building and
leaps to her death.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:15.900
Anne's reaction to chomping on someone's head
on the big screen isn't much more than mild

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.480
revulsion — but come on, there are rockets
to stop.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:19.240
Think big picture.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:20.660
Death by chocolate

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.700
Venom has a pretty healthy appetite, which
includes tater tots both frozen and burnt,

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.200
trash chicken, live lobsters and dogs, and
human organs.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.760
"Eyes, lungs, pancreas.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.480
So many snacks, so little time."

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.560
So, it may come as a small surprise when Venom
asks for chocolate at the end of the film.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.320
But yes, that's also from the comics.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:48.140
In the Venom: The Hunger miniseries, Marvel's
number one gloopy boy expresses a distaste

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:51.570
for normal, human food, and his hunger for
human brains awakens.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:55.190
It's not just a gritty 1990s comic book choice,
it's science.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:59.250
The thing that the Venom symbiote actually
craves from brains is phenylethylamine, a

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:00.520
human neurotransmitter.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:04.220
Coincidentally, that organic compound is also
found in chocolate.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:08.250
The compound also happens to be in various
types of shellfish, so those raw lobsters

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:10.340
Eddie chowed down on were a pretty good choice,
too.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:11.960
Stan the Man, again

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.700
It's no surprise that Stan Lee makes one of
his trademark cameos in Venom, walking a small

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:18.000
dog near the end of the movie.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:22.940
Stan is more or less a minor hero in the MCU,
as well as the Spider-verse, keeping children

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:27.540
safe on school buses, delivering packages
safely, and even keeping casino chips safe

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.340
for easily-distracted heroes.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:30.440
"You know what?

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:37.020
I think I'll just take these, bring them over
here, and hold on for safe keeping."

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.620
He's so great that he has stories to tell
the Watchers, who literally know everything

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:43.420
that ever happened and will happen.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:47.120
Giving Eddie Brock advice on love is just
the most recent of his heroic feats.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:48.300
Carnage

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:52.340
Of course, there's the end-credits appearance
of Woody Harrelson's Cletus Kasady.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:56.260
Comic fans already know that Kasady becomes
Carnage, a pretty violent villain and one

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.180
of many symbiotes in the Marvel Universe.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:02.220
Whether or not a wandering symbiote found
its way into Kasady by the end of Venom is

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:06.090
unclear, though it stands to reason that if
something as wickedly powerful as Carnage

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:09.140
was present, Kasady wouldn't be quietly sitting
in a cell.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:12.940
The symbiote's signature move is having razor-sharp
blades for hands, after all.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:14.300
Those straps mean nothing.

